FILE: app/src/orbit-camera.ts
```ts
import {
  createArcRotateCamera,
  attachControl,
  enableArcRotateKeyboardControls,
  type ArcRotateCamera,
  type SceneContext,
} from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function createOrbitCamera(canvas: HTMLCanvasElement, scene: SceneContext): ArcRotateCamera {
  const camera = createArcRotateCamera(0, Math.PI / 4, 10, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 });
  enableArcRotateKeyboardControls();
  attachControl(camera, canvas, scene, { keyboard: true });
  return camera;
}
```

I created `src/orbit-camera.ts` which builds an `ArcRotateCamera` targeting the origin at radius 10 (alpha 0, beta π/4 for a pleasant 3/4 view), calls `enableArcRotateKeyboardControls()` (required per note 10 before the `keyboard: true` option is honoured), and then attaches the library's pointer + keyboard controls via `attachControl`. The only thing I was unsure of is whether all of those symbols are re-exported from the `@babylonjs/lite` package root versus needing subpath imports like `@babylonjs/lite/camera/arc-rotate`; I assumed a flat root export since you described it as a single package import.
